    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-3661",
      "problem": "The graph below shows a section of the function $y=\\dfrac{1}{3}\\cot{(x+C)},$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} <            C <            \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ what is the value of $C?$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14256901/assets/140eb5ba37908504b1976eefb29cf3515569314b28bc7a752d3d705e27702a58.png)",
      "worked_solution": "The constant $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function $y=\\dfrac 1 3\\cot x.$\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the given curve shifted **left** by $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ units. So, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac 1 3\\cot\\left(x+\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $C = \\dfrac{1}{2}.$",
      "html_path": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14256901/observations/step-e3661-423415ef67bd4d12799a.html",
